### Can Java achieve multiple inheritance?

Java disallows multiple class inheritance but allows multiple interface implementation.

### What is the diamond problem?

```java
// Hypothetical: if Java allowed multiple class inheritance
class Vehicle {
    public void start() { System.out.println("Vehicle starting"); }
}
class ElectricVehicle extends Vehicle {
    @Override public void start() { System.out.println("Electric motor starting silently"); }
}
class GasVehicle extends Vehicle {
    @Override public void start() { System.out.println("Gas engine starting with roar"); }
}
// ILLEGAL in Java:
// class HybridVehicle extends ElectricVehicle, GasVehicle { }
// h.start(); // which start()? Electric's silent? Gas's roar? Ambiguous.
//
//        Vehicle
//       /      \
// Electric    Gas
//       \      /
//       Hybrid    <- which start()?
```

### How do interfaces solve this?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface Tradable {
    void executeTrade();
    default String getMarket() { return "NSE"; }
}

public interface Reportable {
    void generateReport();
    default String getMarket() { return "BSE"; } // same default method signature
}

public class TradingService implements Tradable, Reportable {
    @Override public void executeTrade() { System.out.println("Trade executed"); }
    @Override public void generateReport() { System.out.println("Report generated"); }

    // Conflict: both interfaces have getMarket() default
    // Compile error if not overridden: "inherits unrelated defaults for getMarket()"
    @Override
    public String getMarket() {
        return Tradable.super.getMarket(); // delegate to Tradable's version: "NSE"
        // Or: return Reportable.super.getMarket(); // delegate to Reportable's: "BSE"
        // Or: return "NSE + BSE"; // own implementation
    }
}
```

### What is the resolution priority?

```java
// Priority: class method > sub-interface default > super-interface default

public interface Auditable {
    default String audit() { return "Interface audit"; }
}

public class BaseService {
    public String audit() { return "Class audit"; }
}

public class TradingService extends BaseService implements Auditable {
    // No override needed. BaseService.audit() wins over Auditable.audit().
}

new TradingService().audit(); // "Class audit" (class wins)
```

### Why do interfaces avoid the diamond problem?

- Abstract methods have no body: no ambiguity (class provides the body).
- Default methods: if conflict, compile error forces class to resolve.
- Static methods: not inherited, no conflict possible.
- Class always wins: if class has the method, it overrides all interface defaults.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Java's approach is safe: conflicts are always resolved explicitly by the programmer.
- The InterfaceName.super.method() syntax only works inside the implementing class.
- Sealed interfaces (Java 17) further control which classes can implement them.
